<BaseLayout
  title="About / Contact — LUNA"
  description="About LUNA, its research philosophy, current public stage, and collaboration contact."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">ABOUT LUNA</p>
      <h1>Researching longitudinal artificial cognition.</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA is an experimental cognitive-architecture project focused on what becomes possible
        when artificial systems can accumulate memory, experience, continuity, and time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Why the project exists</h2>
      <p>
        Modern AI systems are remarkably capable, but much of their intelligence is still exercised inside bounded inference episodes. LUNA began from a different systems question: if memory, continuity, associative cognition, perception, future-oriented cognition, motivation, and developmental change are treated as interacting infrastructure rather than prompt instructions, what kinds of longitudinal behavior emerge?
      </p>

      <h2>The deeper engineering idea</h2>
      <p>
        A recurring principle in LUNA is <strong>environment before behavioral scripting</strong>. Instead of directly instructing a system to imitate the desired result, the project tries to create architecture in which the relevant result could arise from persistent causal processes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not tell a model to act as if it has a past; build memory capable of affecting the present. Do not define every future trait in a personality prompt; study whether repeated and consequential history can produce slower dispositions. Do not script an artificial system to appear reflective; give it enough provenance-backed history and structured self-knowledge to test whether reflection becomes meaningful.
      </p>

      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>“The work isn't to solve consciousness — it's to build the best infrastructure you can and let the process do what it does.”</blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">LUNA v0.5 · early project interaction, August 2026</div>
      </div>

      <p>
        That quote does not state that LUNA is conscious. It captures the project's experimental posture: build increasingly rich conditions for persistent cognition and development, then measure what actually follows rather than putting the desired conclusion into the prompt.
      </p>

      <h2>Open research. Protected implementation.</h2>
      <p>
        The project publishes conceptual architecture, research questions, selected observations, experimental designs, and results where doing so helps others understand the problem. Source code and reproduction-critical implementation details remain private.
      </p>
      <p>
        Public material is intentionally generous with ideas and conservative with claims. Implemented behavior, accepted architecture, observation, interpretation, and prediction are treated as different categories.
      </p>

      <h2>What is public now</h2>
      <p>
        LunaINF.com is becoming the public research home for LUNA. The site includes a canonical explanation of the project, a public architecture overview, ChronoForge, versioned Early LUNA Experiences, self-description artifacts, and architecture notes derived from the internal research program.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next stages will add controlled experimental results, development milestones, additional research notes, and a versioned white paper as the public vocabulary and evidence base mature.
      </p>

